'''Musashi Dairokuten Shrine''' (武蔵第六天神社) is a Shinto shrine located in {{ill|Saitama Prefecture|ja|埼玉県}}, {{ill|Saitama City|ja|さいたま市}}, {{ill|Iwatsuki Ward|ja|岩槻区}}.



{{ill|Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|en}} and {{ill|Dairokuten Sakaki Shrine|ja|第六天榊神社}} are officially registered as simply "Dairokuten Shrine" and "Sakaki Jinja," respectively. 
[[sn:Musashi Dairokuten Shrine]]
{{DEFAULTSORT:Musashi Dairokuten Shrine}}
[[sn:Musashi Dairokuten Shrine]]
{{DEFAULTSORT:Musashi Dairokuten Shrine}}
[[ja:武蔵第六天神社]]


== History ==
During the period of {{ill|Shinbutsu-shūgō|ja|神仏習合}}, this shrine was one of the {{ill|Sixth Heavenly Deity Shrine|ja|第六天神社}} that enshrined the {{ill|Sixth Heavenly Demon King|ja|第六天魔王}} ({{ill|Tataka Jizaiten|ja|他化自在天}}), and was founded in {{ill|1782|ja|1782年}} (the 2nd year of the {{ill|Tenmei Era|ja|天明}}). However, even before that it existed as a {{ill|hokora|ja|祠}}, and during the {{ill|Sengoku Period (Japan)|ja|戦国時代 (日本)|lt=Sengoku Period|lt_ja=戦国時代}} it attracted the faith of local residents, including the lords of the {{ill|Ota Clan|ja|太田氏}}.<ref name="Atsha">[https://dairokuten.or.jp/yuisho/ {{ill|History|ja|御由緒}}] {{ill|Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|ja|武蔵第六天神社}}</ref>

This shrine is believed to dispel all misfortunes and to ensure the safety of the household and abundant harvests. Because its {{ill|Merit|ja|功徳|lt=Benefit|lt_ja=ご利益}} extend over a wide range, many pilgrims visit not only from the former {{ill|Musashi Province|ja|武蔵国}} ({{ill|Saitama Prefecture|ja|埼玉県}} and {{ill|Tokyo Metropolis|ja|東京都}}) but from throughout the entire {{ill|Kanto Region|ja|関東地方|lt=Kanto Area|lt_ja=関東一円}}. In addition, due to the enshrined deity’s “{{ill|Enshrined Deity|ja|祭神}}” known as “Men'''ash'''ison”, which is venerated as a god who cures foot ailments, the shrine has also recently come to be regarded as a guardian of {{ill|Traffic Safety|ja|交通安全}}.<ref name="Atsha"></ref><ref name="Sanpo">Oomura, Susumu, 『Saitama Furusato Sanpo (Iwatsuki City)』, Sakitama Shuppankai, 1992, 111p</ref>

Under the {{ill|Meiji Era|ja|明治}}’s {{ill|Modern Shrine Ranking System|ja|近代社格制度}}, the shrine was originally classified as a “mukaku-sha” (unranked shrine). However, in {{ill|1907|ja|1907年}} (the 40th year of the Meiji Era), four shrines—including the village shrine {{ill|Katori Shrine|ja|香取神社}}—were enshrined together, and it became a village shrine.<ref name="Atsha"></ref>

Because many pilgrims visit, there are many restaurants serving river fish dishes in the vicinity of this shrine.<ref name="Sanpo"></ref>

<gallery>
File:Road to Musashi Dairokuten Jinja.JPG|The road leading to this shrine (with river fish restaurants)
</gallery>

