'''Musashi Dairokuten Shrine''' (武蔵第六天神社) is a Shinto shrine located in {{ill|Saitama Prefecture|ja|埼玉県|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Saitama Prefecture|qq=}}, {{ill|Saitama City|ja|さいたま市|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Saitama City|qq=}}, {{ill|Iwatsuki Ward|ja|岩槻区|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Iwatsuki Ward|qq=}}.
{{ill|Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|en|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|qq=}} and {{ill|Dairokuten Sakaki Shrine|ja|第六天榊神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Dairokuten Sakaki Shrine|qq=}} are officially registered as simply "Dairokuten Shrine" and "Sakaki Jinja," respectively. 

== History ==
During the period of {{ill|Shinbutsu-shūgō|ja|神仏習合|1=Shinbutsu-shūgō|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinbutsu-shūgō|13=User:Immanuelle/Shinbutsu-shūgō|qq=}}, this shrine was one of the {{ill|Sixth Heavenly Deity Shrine|ja|第六天神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sixth Heavenly Deity Shrine|qq=}} that enshrined the {{ill|Sixth Heavenly Demon King|ja|第六天魔王|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sixth Heavenly Demon King|qq=}} ({{ill|Tataka Jizaiten|ja|他化自在天|1=Takijizaiten|2=ja|3=Takijizaiten|4=zh|5=他化自在天|6=th|7=ปรนิมมิตวสวัตดี|8=ru|9=Паринимитра-вашавартин|10=ပရနိမ္မိတဝသဝတ္တီဘုံ|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tataka Jizaiten|qq=}}), and was founded in {{ill|1782|ja|1782nd year|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1782|qq=}} (the 2nd year of the {{ill|Tenmei Era|ja|天明|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tenmei Era|qq=}}). However, even before that it existed as a {{ill|hokora|ja|祠|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/hokora|qq=}}, and during the {{ill|Sengoku Period (Japan)|ja|戦国時代 (日本)|lt=Sengoku Period|lt_ja=戦国時代|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sengoku Period (Japan)|qq=}} it attracted the faith of local residents, including the lords of the {{ill|Ota Clan|ja|太田氏|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Ota Clan|qq=}}.<ref name="Atsha">[https://dairokuten.or.jp/yuisho/ {{ill|History|ja|御由緒|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/History|qq=}}] {{ill|Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|ja|武蔵第六天神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|qq=}}</ref>

This shrine is believed to dispel all misfortunes and to ensure the safety of the household and abundant harvests. Because its {{ill|Merit|ja|功徳|lt=Benefit|lt_ja=ご利益|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Merit|qq=}} extend over a wide range, many pilgrims visit not only from the former {{ill|Musashi Province|ja|武蔵国|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Musashi Province|qq=}} ({{ill|Saitama Prefecture|ja|埼玉県|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Saitama Prefecture|qq=}} and {{ill|Tokyo Metropolis|ja|東京都|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tokyo Metropolis|qq=}}) but from throughout the entire {{ill|Kanto Region|ja|関東地方|lt=Kanto Area|lt_ja=関東一円|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Kanto Region|qq=}}. In addition, due to the enshrined deity’s “{{ill|Enshrined Deity|ja|祭神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Enshrined Deity|qq=}}” known as “Men'''ash'''ison”, which is venerated as a god who cures foot ailments, the shrine has also recently come to be regarded as a guardian of {{ill|Traffic Safety|ja|交通安全|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Traffic Safety|qq=}}.<ref name="Atsha"></ref><ref name="Sanpo">Oomura, Susumu, 『Saitama Furusato Sanpo (Iwatsuki City)』, Sakitama Shuppankai, 1992, 111p</ref>

Under the {{ill|Meiji Era|ja|明治|1=Meiji era|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiji Era|13=User:Immanuelle/Meiji era|qq=}}’s {{ill|Modern Shrine Ranking System|ja|近代社格制度|1=Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|1=Modern system of Ranked Shinto shrines|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Modern Shrine Ranking System|13=User:Immanuelle/Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|qq=}}, the shrine was originally classified as a “mukaku-sha” (unranked shrine). However, in {{ill|1907|ja|1907年|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/1907|qq=}} (the 40th year of the Meiji Era), four shrines—including the village shrine {{ill|Katori Jingu|lt=Katori Jingu|lt=Katori Shrine|ja|香取神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Katori Shrine|qq=}}—were enshrined together, and it became a village shrine.<ref name="Atsha"></ref>

Because many pilgrims visit, there are many restaurants serving river fish dishes in the vicinity of this shrine.<ref name="Sanpo"></ref>

