'''Musashi Dairokuten Shrine''' (武蔵第六天神社) is a Shinto shrine located in [[:en:Saitama Prefecture|Saitama Prefecture]], [[:en:Saitama (city)|Saitama City]], [[:en:Iwatsuki-ku, Saitama|Iwatsuki Ward]].
{{ill|Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|en|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|qq=}} and {{ill|Dairokuten Sakaki Shrine|ja|第六天榊神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Dairokuten Sakaki Shrine|qq=}} are officially registered as simply "Dairokuten Shrine" and "Sakaki Jinja," respectively. 

== History ==
During the period of [[:en:Shinbutsu-shūgō|Shinbutsu-shūgō]], this shrine was one of the {{ill|Sixth Heavenly Deity Shrine|ja|第六天神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sixth Heavenly Deity Shrine|qq=}} that enshrined the {{ill|Sixth Heavenly Demon King|ja|第六天魔王|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Sixth Heavenly Demon King|qq=}} ({{ill|Tataka Jizaiten|ja|他化自在天|1=Takijizaiten|2=ja|3=Takijizaiten|4=zh|5=他化自在天|6=th|7=ปรนิมมิตวสวัตดี|8=ru|9=Паринимитра-вашавартин|10=ပရနိမ္မိတဝသဝတ္တီဘုံ|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Tataka Jizaiten|qq=}}), and was founded in [[:en:1782|1782]] (the 2nd year of the [[:en:Tenmei|Tenmei Era]]). However, even before that it existed as a [[:en:Hokora|hokora]], and during the [[:en:Sengoku period|Sengoku Period]] it attracted the faith of local residents, including the lords of the [[:en:Ōta clan|Ota Clan]].<ref name="Atsha">[https://dairokuten.or.jp/yuisho/ {{bad link|These are causing so much issues|History|ja|御由緒|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/History|qq=}}] {{ill|Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|ja|武蔵第六天神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Musashi Dairokuten Shrine|qq=}}</ref>

This shrine is believed to dispel all misfortunes and to ensure the safety of the household and abundant harvests. Because its [[:en:Merit (Buddhism)|Benefit]] extend over a wide range, many pilgrims visit not only from the former [[:en:Musashi Province|Musashi Province]] ([[:en:Saitama Prefecture|Saitama Prefecture]] and [[:en:Tokyo|Tokyo Metropolis]]) but from throughout the entire [[:en:Kantō region|Kanto Area]]. In addition, due to the enshrined deity’s “{{ill|Enshrined Deity|ja|祭神|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Enshrined Deity|qq=}}” known as “Men'''ash'''ison”, which is venerated as a god who cures foot ailments, the shrine has also recently come to be regarded as a guardian of [[:en:Road traffic safety|Traffic Safety]].<ref name="Atsha"></ref><ref name="Sanpo">Oomura, Susumu, 『Saitama Furusato Sanpo (Iwatsuki City)』, Sakitama Shuppankai, 1992, 111p</ref>

Under the [[:en:Meiji era|Meiji era]]’s [[:en:Modern system of ranked Shinto shrines|Modern system of Ranked Shinto shrines]], the shrine was originally classified as a “mukaku-sha” (unranked shrine). However, in [[:en:1907|1907]] (the 40th year of the Meiji Era), four shrines—including the village shrine {{ill|Katori Jingu|lt=Katori Jingu|lt=Katori Shrine|ja|香取神社|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Katori Shrine|qq=}}—were enshrined together, and it became a village shrine.<ref name="Atsha"></ref>

Because many pilgrims visit, there are many restaurants serving river fish dishes in the vicinity of this shrine.<ref name="Sanpo"></ref>

<gallery>
File:Road to Musashi Dairokuten Jinja.JPG|The road leading to this shrine (with river fish restaurants)
</gallery>

== Transportation Access ==
* A 13‑minute walk from the [[:en:Transit bus|Route Bus]] stop at {{ill|Oodo Bus Stop|ja|大戸停留所|qq=draft|12=simple|13=User:Immanuelle/Oodo Bus Stop|qq=}}.

